WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.380
- Hey guys, you requested House MD.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.290
- Do I get bonus points
if I act like I care?

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.458
- Let's get started.

00:00:05.458 --> 00:00:08.125
(relaxed music)

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.680
This reminds me of where
I grew up in Brooklyn.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:15.899
Or is this a jail?

00:00:15.899 --> 00:00:17.300
(laughing)

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.380
- Why are you late?

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.710
- I feel like you won't like the answer.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.130
- I already know the answer.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.240
- I missed the bus.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.440
- I don't doubt it, no
bus stops near Brad's?

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.570
- Every medical show has to start off

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.700
with some kind of sexual thing.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.810
Medicine is so exciting as it is,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.710
why does there have to be sex?

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.420
Calm down, stop having sex for a second.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.600
- You should never keep
anything from your parents.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.289
And I told mine- (slurring)

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:41.675
(kids laughing)

00:00:41.675 --> 00:00:42.940
(sighing)

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:43.773
(slurring)

00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:46.289
(kids laughing)

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:47.522
- Oh.

00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:50.355
(slurring)

00:00:50.355 --> 00:00:53.370
(kids laughing)

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.093
- Are we having a stroke?

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.619
Slurred speech, classic sign of a stroke.

00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:02.210
(slurring)

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.713
Oh, problems with moving the arms.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.070
- C

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.116
- Call 911

00:01:09.116 --> 00:01:12.126
- A-L

00:01:12.126 --> 00:01:13.852
H-E

00:01:13.852 --> 00:01:14.993
The!

00:01:14.993 --> 00:01:16.910
We know that word, the!

00:01:22.737 --> 00:01:23.570
- Oh no!

00:01:25.724 --> 00:01:27.297
(grunting)

00:01:27.297 --> 00:01:28.583
- Oh, that's a seizure.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.030
She's having a general
tonic-clonic seizure.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:33.470
- [James] 29 year old female,

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.830
first seizure, one month ago,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.490
lost the ability to speak.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.060
Babbled like a baby,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.470
aggressive deterioration of mental status.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.580
- [House] See that, they all
assume that I'm a patient

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.020
because of this cane.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.620
- [James] So put on a white
coat like the rest of us.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.640
- [House] I don't want
them to think I'm a doctor.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.780
- [James] You see where the administration

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.240
might have a problem with that attitude?

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.890
- [House] People don't want a sick doctor.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.700
- Not wearing your white coat,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.210
I guess it's not really a big deal

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.960
because sometimes I go
without my white coat

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.530
and just wear my stethoscope.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.670
It's really for infectious purposes only,

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.730
so that if you get any
kind of germs on you,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.510
you don't bring it back
home to your family.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.540
Or if, obviously, some
blood splatters on you

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.670
or some other bodily fluid, I guess.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.620
- Brain tumor, she's gonna die, boring.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:13.980
- That was a little cold.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.670
- She's 29, whatever she's
got is highly unlikely.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.400
- Protein markers for
the three most prevalent

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.710
brain cancers came up negative.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.000
- Tumor markers for brain cancer?

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.520
That's not how we diagnose brain tumors.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:25.700
We use imaging.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.490
That's the gold standard for brain tumors,

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.850
obviously in addition to biopsy if needed,

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:34.960
but tumor markers are not
a way to rule out a cancer.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.720
- And she's not responding
to radiation treatment.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.740
- None of which is even
close to this positive.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.600
- Medically, I'm very confused.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.440
There's a patient who had a seizure.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.290
They think that she has a tumor,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.680
they did some lab work.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.610
Doesn't show she has a tumor.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.650
They're giving her radiation even though

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.070
that they don't see a tumor.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.810
I could not be more confused right now,

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.360
or this totally doesn't
make sense medically.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.590
- Shouldn't we be speaking to the patient

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:58.940
before we start diagnosing?

00:03:00.399 --> 00:03:01.232
- Is she a doctor?

00:03:01.232 --> 00:03:02.065
- No, but--

00:03:02.065 --> 00:03:03.400
- Everybody lies.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.430
- Doctor House doesn't
like dealing with patients.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.777
- Isn't treating patients
why we became doctors?

00:03:07.777 --> 00:03:10.470
- No, treating illnesses
is why we became doctors.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.330
Treating patients is what
makes most doctors miserable.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.120
- So you're trying to
eliminate the humanity

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.910
from the practice of medicine?

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.650
- If we don't talk to
them, they can't lie to us.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.710
- Excuse me Doctor House.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.560
You can have a good
conversation with patients.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.280
You don't need to believe
every word they say.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.290
You can be honest, you
can say you don't know,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.380
but give a plan of what you're
doing about not knowing.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.590
So basically how you're
getting to the answer.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:33.477
- First thing of medical school,

00:03:33.477 --> 00:03:35.580
"If you hear hoofbeats, you
think horses, not zebras."

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:36.820
- Very common phrase.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:37.653
- Medical school.

00:03:37.653 --> 00:03:39.803
- That and Treton could have
screwed up the blood test.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.590
I assume its a corollary
that if people lie

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:43.913
that people screw up.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.560
- Redraw the blood tests
and get her scheduled

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.320
for that contrast MRI ASAP.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.090
Let's find out what kind of
zebra we're treating here.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.630
- So a contrast MRI
basically allows you to see

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.600
what blood vessels are
doing within the brain.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.550
The fact that they're going to a lab

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.610
and questioning if the lab is accurate,

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.230
we do do that occasionally,

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.040
but that's not the first
thing we think about.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.040
I guess 'cause this case is more complex.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.620
- I was expecting you in
my office 20 minutes ago.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.540
- Really? Well that's odd,
because I had no intention

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.910
of being in your office 20 minutes ago.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.490
- You think we have nothing to talk about?

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:15.500
- No, just can't think of
anything I'd be interested in.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.664
- I sign your paychecks.

00:04:16.664 --> 00:04:17.914
- I got tenure.

00:04:20.141 --> 00:04:21.910
- I can still fire you if
you're not doing your job.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:22.880
- I'm here from nine to five.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.989
- Your billings are
practically non-existent.

00:04:24.989 --> 00:04:25.822
- Rough year.

00:04:25.822 --> 00:04:27.410
- You ignore requests for consults.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:28.243
- I call back sometimes I misdial.

00:04:28.243 --> 00:04:30.180
- You're six years behind

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.550
on your obligations to this clinic.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:34.030
- See, I was right, this
doesn't interest me.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:34.910
- Six years.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.060
Times three weeks.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.610
You owe me better than four months.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.500
- It's five o'clock, I'm going home.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.460
- Doctors don't work nine to five's.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.900
We have crazy schedules.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.850
If someone that's more senior to us,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.840
obviously like our boss,
like she is in this case,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.190
tells you to get something
done, you're getting it done.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.070
But also because we
have a love for the job.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:53.090
We wanna treat patients,

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.400
I don't know why he's
being this sarcastic guy,

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.733
but I will say it's really funny.

00:04:56.733 --> 00:04:58.270
- I'm angry!

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.880
You're risking a patients life!

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.340
- I assume those are two separate points.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.630
- You showed me disrespect,
you embarrassed me

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.070
and as long as I work here you have no--

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.960
- Is the yelling designed to scare me,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.300
because I'm not sure--
- I love her.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.580
- What it is I'm supposed to be scared of.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.090
More yelling?

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.810
That's not scary.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:14.650
That you're gonna hurt me?

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.883
That's scary, but I'm
pretty sure I can outrun ya.

00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:19.050
(yelling)

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.413
- 'Kay.

00:05:21.413 --> 00:05:23.540
That's cool that he's
popping pills on the job.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.543
Oh this is in Jersey, Plainsboro.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.350
I work in New Jersey.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.250
- All right Rebecca,

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.480
I know you may feel a little
claustrophobic in there,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.040
but we need you to remain still.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.180
- It's very true that people
get claustrophobic in MRI's,

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.420
you have to lay there for
an extended period of time.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.050
The noise is very loud.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.010
So there are times that I have
to pre-medicate my patients

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.780
and give them a dose of a benzodiazepines,

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.270
like Xanax, most commonly,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.848
that you're probably familiar with.

00:05:49.848 --> 00:05:51.440
(loud clicking)

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.005
Yeah it's really loud,
it's even louder than this.

00:05:54.005 --> 00:05:56.588
(loud banging)

00:05:59.164 --> 00:06:00.390
- I don't feel so good.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.640
- [Doctor] It's all
right, just try to relax.

00:06:03.947 --> 00:06:06.076
(loud whirring)

00:06:06.076 --> 00:06:07.480
(choking)

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.280
- Oh, is she having another seizure?

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:12.621
- Rebecca?

00:06:12.621 --> 00:06:14.690
(choking)

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:15.523
Rebecca?

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:17.943
- Is she choking?

00:06:19.236 --> 00:06:21.403
(choking)

00:06:23.191 --> 00:06:26.382
- Is she having an anaphylactic
attack to the contrast?

00:06:26.382 --> 00:06:27.215
- Rebecca?

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:29.730
Get her out of there.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.500
- She probably fell
asleep, she's exhausted.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.970
- She was claustrophobic 30 seconds ago,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.574
she's not sleeping, we
gotta get her out of there.

00:06:34.574 --> 00:06:35.407
- It'll just be another minute.

00:06:35.407 --> 00:06:36.700
- She's having allergic
reaction to gadolinium,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:37.848
she'll be dead in two minutes.

00:06:37.848 --> 00:06:39.430
- Oh no!

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:42.263
Get her out, Epipen into
the upper thigh, quick!

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.033
- She's ashen.

00:06:46.514 --> 00:06:48.841
- She's not breathing, (mumbling)

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:49.723
- Come on!

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:52.450
I can't ventilate.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:56.823
- Too much edema.

00:06:56.823 --> 00:06:57.800
Where's the surgical airway tube.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.220
- You're gonna need to go in the neck

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.815
and do a tracheostomy.

00:07:00.815 --> 00:07:03.648
(heavy breathing)

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.084
- Did he give her the
Epi? I didn't see it.

00:07:09.084 --> 00:07:11.751
(intense music)

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.143
Okay that's way too big of an incision.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.420
Okay, not exactly how that goes.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.600
If you're allergic to the contrast

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.030
that they gave for the MRI,

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.610
it's possible that you can
get this anaphylactic attack,

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:33.630
which means that your
airway starts closing down.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.290
Basically your throat
swells up to the point

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.120
where you can no longer oxygenate.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:38.230
Some people also vomit,

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.680
some people get very nauseous,
blood pressure drops.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.490
Her airway closed up to the
point where she couldn't breathe

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.760
but because it's her throat
closing up and swelling,

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.180
if you make an incision
in the trachea right here

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.300
where there's a soft
little spot for an opening,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.560
you can continue helping
the patient breathe.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:55.860
I'm gonna call some inaccuracies here.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.430
If a patient turns blue like that,

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:58.660
you're hitting a code blue.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:00.280
There's an emergency team coming,

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:02.660
nurses, doctors, respiratory therapists.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:04.080
You also gotta get IV's set,

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.640
you gotta figure out why
the patients heart stopped

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:07.840
or stopped breathing.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.350
There's a lot more that has to happen.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.090
You don't just automatically assume

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.840
it has something to do with
the contrast that you gave.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.280
And he's still popping pills!

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.113
What is he popping?

00:08:16.113 --> 00:08:17.505
I hope those are Advil.

00:08:17.505 --> 00:08:19.720
- You actually want me
to talk to the patient?

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.429
Get a history?

00:08:21.429 --> 00:08:22.640
- We need to know if there's some genetic

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:23.750
or environmental cause

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.228
as to generate an inflammatory response.

00:08:26.228 --> 00:08:28.620
- I thought everybody lied?

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.920
- Truth begins in lies.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:31.753
- Okay.

00:08:31.753 --> 00:08:33.120
So why did you tell him not to talk

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.667
to the patient initially Doctor House?

00:08:35.667 --> 00:08:37.520
"Truth begins in lies."

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.310
Just means that in order
to find out the truth,

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:42.220
you have the person lie to you,

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:45.420
and from there you can then
start making a deduction

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.170
on what part they're lying about.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:48.850
So you need to hear the lie.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:50.660
That's why it definitely makes sense

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:52.600
to always talk to patients first,

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:53.590
regardless if you believe

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.903
that they're telling the truth or not.

00:08:54.903 --> 00:08:55.736
- What are you doing?

00:08:55.736 --> 00:08:56.740
- Painkillers.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.840
- Oh, for your leg?

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.560
- No, 'cause they're yummy.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:01.393
You want one?

00:09:01.393 --> 00:09:03.060
They'll make your back feel better.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:03.893
- No, don't do it!

00:09:03.893 --> 00:09:05.383
That's how the opioid
crisis started, House!

00:09:05.383 --> 00:09:08.010
- Why are you so afraid
of making a mistake?

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:09.980
- Because I'm a doctor.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:10.813
- Yes.
- Because,

00:09:10.813 --> 00:09:12.010
When we make mistakes people die.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:12.843
- Yes.

00:09:12.843 --> 00:09:13.676
- Come on.

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:19.893
People used to have more
respect for cripples, you know.

00:09:22.453 --> 00:09:23.403
They didn't really.

00:09:24.563 --> 00:09:27.210
(laughing)

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:28.403
- I can't see.

00:09:30.587 --> 00:09:31.980
- Uh oh.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.067
- I can't see.

00:09:35.177 --> 00:09:37.927
(dramatic music)

00:09:39.416 --> 00:09:41.999
- She's having a seizure again.

00:09:43.271 --> 00:09:44.686
- Little help in here!

00:09:44.686 --> 00:09:47.353
(intense music)

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.180
- So turning a patient on their side

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.140
is the smartest move
because you want them to,

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:54.680
if they're gonna vomit,
they don't aspirate.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.923
- Can you arrange these to tell a story?

00:10:01.429 --> 00:10:03.762
(sad music)

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.040
- This is a type of neurological testing

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.890
to see if the person can
follow a series of events.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.810
- Had a couple headaches last month.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:15.070
Mild fever.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:18.370
Sometimes I can't sleep and
I have trouble concentrating.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.720
- Apparently not while researching

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.830
this stuff on the internet.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.180
- I was thinking it also
might be fibromyalgia.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:28.910
- Excellent diagnosis.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.943
- Is there anything for that?

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.850
- Do you know I think there just might be.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:40.223
I need 36 Vicodin and change for a dollar.

00:10:40.223 --> 00:10:41.460
- And change.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.933
- 36 Vicodin and change for a dollar?

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:55.911
- Oh, placebo!

00:10:55.911 --> 00:10:56.990
(laughing)

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:59.330
He's gonna give him sugar pills.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:01.560
Oh and he's stealing
the Vicodin for himself.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:06.070
Again, obviously Doctor's
don't swap out medications.

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:08.340
If we prescribe something to the patient,

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:09.750
we send an order to pharmacy

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.680
and the pharmacists fill the order,

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.680
and we don't replace it with
sugar pills for placebo effect.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.440
- So you didn't find anything?

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:17.400
- Everything I found was in my--

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:18.760
- You found ham.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:19.593
- Uh oh.

00:11:19.593 --> 00:11:20.660
- So?

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.300
- Where there's ham, there's pork.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:24.850
Where there's pork,
there's neurocysticercosis.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:25.690
- That's the condition.

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:26.870
Bum bum bam!

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:28.500
Tapeworms!

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:29.333
- It fits.

00:11:29.333 --> 00:11:30.166
It's perfect.

00:11:30.166 --> 00:11:31.180
It explains everything.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.250
- But it proves nothing.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:33.690
- I can prove it by treating it.

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:34.523
- No, you can't.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:38.063
I was just with her.

00:11:38.063 --> 00:11:39.580
She doesn't want any more treatments,

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.300
she doesn't want any more experiments.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:42.960
She wants to go home and die.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.970
- That's called the principle of autonomy.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.410
Basically that a patient has the right

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:50.730
to refuse treatment at any point

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.270
as long as they're competent

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:53.990
and by competent it means

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:56.310
that they're able to
understand the consequences

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:57.170
of their actions.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:00.397
So if they say they wanna
go home, and you tell them,

00:12:00.397 --> 00:12:02.320
"You can possible die if you go home."

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:03.700
They can say that back to you

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.220
and understand what that
means, they're free to go home.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:07.420
- You're being an idiot.

00:12:09.651 --> 00:12:13.110
(thunder roaring)

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:15.103
You have a tapeworm in your brain.

00:12:16.394 --> 00:12:18.377
It's not pleasant, but
if we don't do anything,

00:12:18.377 --> 00:12:20.040
you'll be dead by the weekend.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.280
He's right, we should X-Ray her,

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:22.430
but we don't X-Ray her brain,

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:23.693
we X-Ray her leg.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.550
Worms love thigh muscle.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.320
She's got one in her head,

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.270
I guarantee you there's one in her leg.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.920
- Um, I did not know this.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.620
This is beyond my expertise

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:37.683
and probably most Doctors' expertise.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:48.120
- This here is a worm larvae.

00:12:48.279 --> 00:12:50.610
- So if it's in my leg, it's in my brain?

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.310
- Are you looking for a guarantee?

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:53.993
It's there.

00:12:53.993 --> 00:12:55.730
Probably been there six to 10 years.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:57.050
- How does he know that?

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:58.050
- Carbenzop.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:02.050
- He's giving her a de-wormer.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:04.750
- If anyone asks, you have
11 daughters and five sons.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:06.214
- Aw!

00:13:06.214 --> 00:13:07.890
(kids talking)

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.760
- Wait that's 16 students.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.250
They said her class has 20.

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.970
Where are the other five,
why didn't they show up?

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.277
Oh wait, 11 and five,
sorry the other four.

00:13:16.277 --> 00:13:18.337
"We're happy you're not dead."

00:13:18.337 --> 00:13:20.860
- I want a hug and a kiss
from every single one of you

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:21.693
get up here right now!

00:13:21.693 --> 00:13:23.400
- Don't give the kids the worms!

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:24.233
I'm just kidding.

00:13:24.233 --> 00:13:25.400
- Doctor House?

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.463
You have a patient.

00:13:30.989 --> 00:13:33.380
(laughing)

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:34.780
- He says he needs a refill.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:39.106
- Got change for a dollar?

00:13:39.106 --> 00:13:40.820
(laughing)

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:41.653
- That's funny.

00:13:41.653 --> 00:13:45.250
Patient got hooked on them tic tacs.

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:46.090
There we have it.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.390
House episode one, season
one is in the books.

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:51.600
Medical accuracy?

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.090
It's somewhere in the middle range.

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:55.070
These are very rare cases.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:57.700
Almost never seen in the United States,

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:58.930
like pork tapeworm.

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:01.140
Most of our pork is well
cooked so we don't see that.

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:03.900
The way that they go
about practicing medicine

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.900
is probably the most
unrealistic part of it.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:07.540
House is just a maniac,

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:09.030
treating patients without telling them

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:10.000
what he's treating them for,

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.210
breaking into their homes.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:14.160
Crazy, but also crazy entertaining, so.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.680
I think there's gonna be some
very interesting episodes

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:18.450
to review from Doctor House.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:20.530
If you know any of these specific episodes

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:22.560
that carry a good mystery
and a good balance

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.570
of medical stuff and drama stuff,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.140
leave it down below in the comments.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:28.150
If you want me to do a different show,

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.500
definitely leave it in the comments.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.640
The more subscribers we get, again,

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:34.230
the better and more content
I can continue making.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:36.420
Subscribe, subscribe, subscribe.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:37.844
Stay happy and healthy.

00:14:37.844 --> 00:14:40.511
(relaxed music)

